# Build context: default-deny.
#
# The usual .dockerignore is a denylist, which means every file added to the
# repository from now on is in the build context until somebody remembers to
# exclude it. That is the wrong default for a repository whose threat model is
# "a credential must never end up somewhere it can be copied out of", and a
# build context is exactly such a place: it is uploaded to the daemon whole,
# and anything a COPY reaches lands in a layer that ships.
#
# So: exclude everything, then name the four things that build the package.
# Adding a new source directory without listing it here fails the build loudly
# at `npm run build`, which is the failure mode you want.
*

# --- what the build actually needs ---------------------------------------

# The manifests. `npm ci` refuses to run without the lockfile, which is the
# point: the image is installed from resolved versions, never re-resolved.
!package.json
!package-lock.json

# The source, including the committed src/generated catalog. It is regenerated
# by `npm run codegen` and verified in CI, not built here.
!src

# tsup reads both. tsconfig.json supplies the target; tsup.config.ts is what
# marks @modelcontextprotocol/sdk external, so the runtime install in the
# Dockerfile depends on this file being present and correct.
!tsconfig.json
!tsup.config.ts

# --- belt and braces ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The rules above re-include whole directories, so a secret dropped inside one
# of them would ride in. These re-exclude the shapes that must never be in a
# build context regardless of where they appear, and they come last because the
# last matching pattern wins.

# Never, under any circumstance. `.env` is not read by this server anyway —
# every credential arrives as an environment variable set by the platform.
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Private key material and certificates. This server never terminates TLS, so
# there is no legitimate reason for one to be in the context.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx

# A local registry file. It is a pointer file by schema and cannot contain a
# token — but it does contain your base URLs, and it belongs to the machine it
# was written on, not to an image.
**/.coolify-mcp.json

# npm pack output, which is a tarball of the package including anything the
# host had lying around.
**/*.tgz
